  "body": "This is partially related to [Issue 4445](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/4445).\r\n\r\nIf you use this HTML:\r\n\r\n    <input name=\"u\" type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"username\" />\r\n\r\nThe [W3C validator](https://validator.w3.org/nu/#textarea) will raise the error:\r\n\r\n    Bad value username for attribute autocomplete on element input:\r\n    The autofill field name username is not allowed in this context.\r\n\r\nAs noted in [Issue 983](https://github.com/validator/validator/issues/983), this is because the \"[username](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/#attr-fe-autocomplete-username)\" autocomplete value is from the \"[Text](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#control-group-text)\" group (which includes hidden/text/search), not \"[E-mail](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#control-group-e-mail)\".\r\n\r\nIn comparison, Safari explicitly suggests using `type=\"email\" autocomplete=\"username\"`:\r\n\r\nhttps://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/password_autofill/enabling_password_autofill_on_an_html_input_element\r\n\r\nAnd Chrome suggests `type=\"text\" autocomplete=\"username\"` for their \"Email First Sign-in Flow\":\r\n\r\nhttps://www.chromium.org/developers/design-documents/form-styles-that-chromium-understands\r\n\r\nThis setup helps pair the username with the password (ref password managers), while `type=\"email\"` allows onscreen keyboards to provide an easy to access \"@\" key (`inputmode=\"email\"` has issues), and it can hint to browsers that they should autofill the users email address when on a registration page.",
